Role: Senior System Architect Task: Fix "Split-Brain" Projectile Data Issue in GameEngine Context: The user is experiencing an "Invisible Projectile" bug.

CombatSystem (combat.ts) stores projectiles in its own this.projectiles array.

GameRenderer (renderer.ts) reads from engine.projectiles.

Root Cause: GameEngine likely initializes projectiles = [] separately, causing the renderer to read an empty list while the combat system updates a disconnected list.

Action: Please refactor engine/game.ts to ensure engine.projectiles serves as a proxy/getter for combat.projectiles, ensuring a Single Source of Truth.

Required Changes in engine/game.ts:

Remove the independent public projectiles: Projectile[] = []; property.

Add a Getter to delegate access to the combat system:

TypeScript

// In GameEngine class
public get projectiles(): Projectile[] {
    return this.combat.projectiles;
}
Alternatively, if strict property usage is required (no getter), verify that in the constructor, you assign the reference:

TypeScript

// In constructor after creating combat system
this.combat = new CombatSystem();
this.projectiles = this.combat.projectiles; // Link references
(Note: Getter is safer/preferred).

Output: Please provide the corrected code for engine/game.ts.

